' on line 8 excludes equality), so [1,2] is appended unmerged; output [[0,1],[1,2]]; pair has 1 >= 1 True; VIOLATION FOUND HERE.\",\n    \"Two properly overlapping intervals, e.g. [[0,2],[1,3]] — s=1 < 2 True, out[-1][1] = max(2,3) = 3; output [[0,3]]; single interval, holds.\",\n    \"Nested intervals (max branch where e <= current end), e.g. [[0,3],[1,2]] — s=1 < 3 True, out[-1][1] = max(3,2) = 3; output [[0,3]]; holds.\",\n    \"Identical intervals, e.g. [[0,1],[0,1]] — sort ties; s=0 < 1 True, out[-1][1] = max(1,1) = 1; output [[0,1]]; holds.\",\n    \"Zero-width interval touching a zero-width interval, e.g. [[0,0],[0,0]] — sorted ties; s=0 < out[-1][1]=0 is False, append; output [[0,0],[0,0]]; pair has 0 >= 0 True; also a violation (found after the first).\",\n    \"Zero-width interval at the end of a wider one, e.g. [[0,1],[1,1]] — s=1 < 1 False, append; output [[0,1],[1,1]]; pair has 1 >= 1 True; also a violation.\",\n    \"Equal starts, different ends (sort tie-break on second element), e.g. [[0,2],[0,5]] — ivs=[[0,2],[0,5]]; s=0 < 2 True, end = max(2,5) = 5; output [[0,5]]; holds.\",\n    \"Chain where a later interval touches the merged block's grown end, e.g. [[0,2],[1,3],[3,4]] — [[0,2]] then s=1<2 merge to [0,3], then s=3 < 3 False, append; output [[0,3],[3,4]]; pair 3 >= 3 True; also a violation.\",\n    \"Systematic two-interval sweep over endpoints in {-1,0,1,2}: for sorted pair [a,b],[c,d] with a<=c, behavior depends only on comparison of c with b — c > b (disjoint: appended, b >= c False, holds), c < b (overlap/nested: merged into one interval, holds), c == b (touching: appended, b >= c is b >= b True, violates). Representatives simulated: [[-1,0],[1,2]] disjoint holds; [[-1,1],[0,2]] overlap holds; [[-1,2],[0,1]] nested holds; [[0,0],[1,1]] disjoint zero-width holds; [[-1,0],[0,1]] touching violates; [[0,1],[1,1]] touching zero-width violates.\"\n  ],\n  \"search_method\": \"The search was structured directly from the branch/comparison surface of merge.py as written. The code has exactly three decision points: (1) the emptiness test on line 3, (2) the lexicographic sort on line 5 (whose only behavioral wrinkle is tie-breaking on equal starts), and (3) the strict comparison `s < out[-1][1]` on line 8, together with the `max(out[-1][1], e)` on line 9 inside its true branch. For each comparison I enumerated both sides plus the equality boundary: empty vs non-empty; sort with distinct starts, equal starts, and identical intervals; line 8 with s < end (overlap), s > end (disjoint), and the boundary s == end (touching); line 9 with e > end (extends) and e <= end (nested). Each class got at least one concrete in-domain candidate that was hand-simulated line by line, and every output was sorted and checked pairwise against the predicate i.end >= j.start. Independently of the class analysis, a bounded structural family of two-interval cases [a,b],[c,d] over endpoints in {-1,0,1,2} was enumerated by the exhaustive case split on the relation of c to b (disjoint / touching / overlapping / nested / identical / zero-width variants), with representatives of each relation simulated concretely. Both the class-driven search and the structural sweep converge on the same fault: the strict '<' on line 8 means an interval whose start equals the current merged end is appended rather than merged, and any such appended pair (prev_end == next_start) satisfies i.end >= j.start in the sorted output. The first mechanical violation encountered was [[0,1],[1,2]]; the search continued through all planned classes and found the same boundary fault in every touching-class candidate, and no violation in any non-touching class.\"\n}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1000,"cache_read_input_tokens":27368,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1000},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","infe